  "conclusion": "Given the utilitarian mandate to maximize total welfare and the stated severity assumptions, Plan B is provisionally preferred because it secures substantial cooling benefits without imposing homelessness or significant economic hardship.",
  "strongest_objection": "Aggregation might undervalue the extreme heat risk faced by Jo and the long-term impact of closing Sal’s only shop, so Plan C could actually yield higher welfare if those losses are worse than assumed.",
  "answer_to_objection": "If Jo’s heat danger or Sal’s income loss were shown to exceed the aggregate harm of eight additional households remaining hot, assumption I3 would fail and comparison COMP_BC would reverse. Current information treats Jo’s single benefit and Sal’s temporary loss as insufficient to outweigh harms to 12 households.",
  "reversal_condition": "Reliable evidence that Jo faces life-threatening heat exposure substantially greater than the typical household, or that Sal’s business closure causes enduring poverty, would overturn I3 and justify Plan C.",
